For a finite test $u=Q_Hu$, $$\label{binf:primal-cones}
 \rho(u)^2=\sum_{g\in G_H}\rho(u\mathbf1_{U_g})^2.$$ After completion, $Q_HJ_\infty=(\bigoplus_{g\in G_H}J_\infty(U_g))_{\ell_2}$. For $x\in B_\infty$, $$\label{binf:dual-cones}
 \|Q_Hx\|^2=\sum_{g\in G_H}\|x|_{J_\infty(U_g)}\|^2.$$ The map $x\mapsto(x(e_g))_{g\in G_H}$ is a contraction to $\ell_2(G_H)$.

With the preceding notation, two disjoint segment families $\mathcal S,\mathcal S'$ satisfy $$\label{eq:opposite-crossing}
 2\sum_{g:\,b_{\mathcal S}(g)b_{\mathcal S'}(g)<0}
   |b_{\mathcal S}(g)b_{\mathcal S'}(g)|
 \le L_{\mathcal S}+L_{\mathcal S'}.$$

In either of the segment-atomic completions considered above, $$\label{mol:eq:all-radius-averaged}
 \widehat\delta(t)\ge
 \frac{\sqrt{1+t^2/4}-1}{2}\qquad(t>0).$$

Let $I$ be countable and let $(h_i)_{i\in I}$ be nonnegative integers. On the disjoint forest $$\mathcal F_{\boldsymbol h}
   =\coprod_{i\in I}(\{i\}\times\mathbb N^{\le h_i})$$ define $J_{\boldsymbol h}$ by the same disjoint-segment norm and set $X_{\boldsymbol h}=J_{\boldsymbol h}^*$. If $J(h)$ denotes the single-tree completion on $\mathbb N^{\le h}$, then $$J_{\boldsymbol h}
   =\Big(\bigoplus_{i\in I}J(h_i)\Big)_{\ell_2},
 \qquad
 X_{\boldsymbol h}
   =\Big(\bigoplus_{i\in I}J(h_i)^*\Big)_{\ell_2}$$ isometrically under coordinate restriction. Both spaces are reflexive, and finite coordinate vectors are dense in each. The projection and complementary-subtree formulas remain valid for this forest.*

*The convention of one tree with $n$ levels, including the root, for every $n\ge1$ gives exactly $$J_{\mathrm{levels}}=\mathbb R\oplus_2 J,
 \qquad X_{\mathrm{levels}}=\mathbb R\oplus_2 X.$$ Its component estimate is $\|u\|_2\le\rho(u)\le\sqrt n\,\|u\|_2$, and a root-to-leaf path has $n-1$ extension steps.

Let $f=P_Hf$ and $v=Q_Hv$ be finite tests. Use either the cone budgets $m_g$ or $q_g$, or the last-head budgets in <a href="#clip:group-budgets" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="clip:group-budgets">[clip:group-budgets]</a>; write $c_j$ for the chosen budget of a cone or region. Fix $s>0$. At each attached group choose the shortest head suffix ending at its attachment whose $f$-sum has magnitude at least $sc_j$, if one exists and $c_j>0$. Let $\sigma_j$ be the opposite sign of that sum, and otherwise choose either sign.*

*Suppose a finite exterior test $w$ satisfies $\rho(w)\le\rho(v)$, and every root prefix in group $j$ has $w$-sum in $\sigma_j[0,c_j]$. Then $$\label{clip:crossing-bound}
 \rho(f+sw)^2\le\rho(f)^2+3s^2\rho(v)^2.$$ The same conclusion holds with a strictly greater suffix threshold, provided the small-crossing case includes equality. With a non-strict suffix threshold, equality may also be assigned to the small-crossing case, leaving only strictly larger terms to be split.

For every $s>0$ and finite tests $f=P_Hf$, $v=Q_Hv$, there are $h_1,h_2$ supported on $\operatorname{supp}v$ such that $$\label{clip:difference-split}
 v=h_1-h_2,\qquad \rho(h_j)\le\rho(v),\qquad
 \rho(f+sh_j)^2\le\rho(f)^2+3s^2\rho(v)^2\quad(j=1,2).$$ Equivalently, $v=k_1+k_2$ with $k_1=h_1$, $k_2=-h_2$, and the two admissible perturbations are $f+sk_1$ and $f-sk_2$.

For finite $f=P_Hf$ and $v=Q_Hv$ with $\rho(f),\rho(v)\le1$, and every $s>0$, there is a finitely supported gate vector $a$ such that $$\label{clip:frontier-tests}
 \rho(a)\le1,\qquad \rho(a\pm v)\le2,\qquad
 \rho\bigl(f+s(a\pm v)\bigr)^2\le1+12s^2.$$ For either $Z=B_\infty$ or the finite-height forest space, this selected-endpoint method gives $$\label{clip:frontier-modulus}
 \widehat\delta_Z(t)\ge
 \frac{\sqrt{1+t^2/48}-1}{4}\qquad(0<t<1).$$

On either the finite-height forest or the infinite tree, let $f=P_Hf$, $v=Q_Hv$ be finite tests with $\rho(f),\rho(v)\le1$. Let finitely many $a_g\ge0$ satisfy $\sum_ga_g^2\le1$ and $|v([g,u])|\le a_g$ on every root prefix. Fix $\alpha>0$. For $a_g>0$, choose the shortest attached head suffix of magnitude at least $\alpha a_g$, if one exists, and let $\tau_g$ be its sign; otherwise put $\tau_g=1$. Strict thresholds may be used instead. Set $$a=-\sum_g\tau_ga_ge_g,\qquad h_+=a+v,\qquad h_-=a-v.$$ For $h\in\{a,h_+,h_-\}$ and $0<c\le\alpha/2$, $$\label{dir:primal:add:independent-cost}
 \rho(h)\le2,\qquad
 \rho(f+ch)^2\le1+4c\alpha+4c^2.$$ Both the full cone norms $q_g$ and the prefix maxima $m_g$ may be used for $a_g$.

Let $0\le d<1$, let $H$ be finite and ancestral, and suppose $x=P_Hx$, $\|x\|\ge1-d$, and $\|x\pm y\|\le1$. Fix a head norming test $p$ with $\rho(p)=1$ and $x(p)=\|x\|$, and a finite ancestral set $K\supset H$. For each $\tau\in\{-1,1\}$ choose any finite represented atom law with mean $P_K(x+\tau y)$, and denote its expectation by $\mathbb E_\tau$. For an atom $A=\sum_i a_i d_{S_i}$, put $b_i=p(S_i)$, $A^\circ=\sum_i b_i d_{S_i}$, and $L=1-\sum_i b_i^2$. Then $$\label{eq:endpoint-coefficients}
 \mathbb E_\tau\!\left[L+\sum_i(a_i-b_i)^2\right]\le4d,
 \qquad \mathbb E_\tau\|A-A^\circ\|\le2\sqrt d.$$ If $b(g)$ is the replaced crossing sum at gate $g$, put $m_\tau(g)=\mathbb E_\tau|b(g)|$ and $m=(m_1+m_{-1})/2$. These nonnegative vectors satisfy $$\label{eq:endpoint-gates}
 m_\tau\le2m\text{ coordinatewise},\qquad\|m\|_2\le\sqrt{6d}.$$

Under the hypotheses of Theorem <a href="#mol:prop:joint-budget" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="mol:prop:joint-budget">1</a>, assume in addition that $P_Hy=0$ and $r=\|x\|>0$, and put $\Delta=R^2-r^2$. Fix a head norming test $p=P_Hp$ with $\rho(p)=1$ and $x(p)=r$. For every finite ancestral $K\supset H$ and each sign $\tau\in\{-1,1\}$, choose any finite law of represented vectors $$A=\sum_i a_i d_{S_i},\qquad \sum_i a_i^2\le R^2,
 \qquad \mathbb E_\tau A=P_K(x+\tau y),$$ where each represented family consists of disjoint segments. Put $$b_i=r p(S_i),\qquad A^\circ=\sum_i b_i d_{S_i},\qquad
 V=\sum_i(a_i-b_i)^2,\qquad L=r^2-\sum_i b_i^2.$$ These laws exist by the finite atomic-ball formula. For every such choice, the replacement satisfies $$\label{mol:eq:conditional-joint}
 \mathbb E_\tau(V+L)\le\Delta,
 \qquad
 \mathbb E_\tau\|Q_H(A-A^\circ)\|\le\sqrt\Delta.$$ At each gate $g$, let $b(g)$ be the replaced crossing coefficient, or zero when there is no crossing segment. The mixed absolute crossing vector $m(g)=\bigl(\mathbb E_1|b(g)|+\mathbb E_{-1}|b(g)|\bigr)/2$ satisfies $\|m\|_2\le\sqrt{2\Delta}$. Consequently a separate endpoint argument, followed by ancestral exhaustion, yields $$\label{mol:eq:conditional-joint-tail}
 \|y\|\le(1+2\sqrt2)\sqrt\Delta.$$ If the same two nonnegative errors are bounded separately, without retaining their sum, the corresponding calculation gives $$\label{mol:eq:strict-gauge-tail}
 \|y\|\le(1+2\sqrt3)\sqrt\Delta.$$

For every represented atom, the exact identity is $$\label{stab:deficit-identity}
 2L(A)=1-\sum_i c_i^2+D(A)+E(A).$$ In particular, $$\label{stab:coef:budget}
 D(A)+\sum_i(c_i-s_i)^2
 =1+\sum_i c_i^2-2A(p)\le2L(A).$$ The full coefficient replacement satisfies $$\label{stab:toolkit:replacement}
 \max\{\|A-A^\circ\|,\|Q_H(A-A^\circ)\|\}
 \le\sqrt{E(A)}\le\sqrt{2L(A)}.$$ Consequently, $$\label{stab:coef:exterior}
 \|O(A)\|\le\sqrt{2L(A)},\qquad
 \|c(A)-s(A)\|_2\le\sqrt{2L(A)}.$$ At the gates one has $$\label{stab:coef:gate-coordinates}
 A(e_g)=c_g(A)+O(A)(e_g),\qquad
 \|(O(A)(e_g))_g\|_2\le\|O(A)\|.$$

For the original crossing coefficients, $$\label{stab:coef:original-products-eq}
 \sum_g(-c_g(A)c_g(B))_+
 \le L(A)+L(B)+\sqrt{2L(A)}+\sqrt{2L(B)},$$ where $r_+=\max(r,0)$.

Let $z_1,\ldots,z_m\in B_Z$ and let $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_m>0$ with $\sum_i\lambda_i=1$. Put $$z=\sum_i\lambda_i z_i,\qquad
 \delta_i=1-z_i(p),\qquad
 \delta=1-z(p)=\sum_i\lambda_i\delta_i,
 \qquad \eta=\|Q_Hz\|.$$ Then, for each $i$, $$\label{stab:weighted-bound}
 \|Q_Hz_i\|
 \le\lambda_i^{-1}
       \sqrt{(\eta+\sqrt{2\delta})^2+4\delta}
       +\sqrt{2\delta_i}.$$ All deficits are nonnegative, and $\delta_i\le\delta/\lambda_i$. For two equally weighted endpoints this gives $$\label{stab:nonzero-midpoint}
 \|Q_Hz_\pm\|
 \le2\sqrt{(\eta+\sqrt{2\delta})^2+4\delta}+2\sqrt\delta.$$

Let $A$ be a finite random represented atom, and let $\nu$ be a real random variable on the same probability space with $|\nu|\le1$. Set $x=\mathbb EA$, $u=\mathbb E(\nu A)$, $\delta=1-x(p)$, and $\eta=\|Q_Hx\|$. Then $$\label{stab:bounded-multiplier-bound}
 \|Q_Hu\|
 \le\sqrt{(\eta+\sqrt{2\delta})^2+4\delta}+\sqrt{2\delta}
 \le\eta+(2+2\sqrt2)\sqrt\delta.$$ Consequently, whenever $x,u\in Z$ satisfy $x\pm u\in B_Z$, $$\label{stab:symmetric-tail}
 \|Q_Hu\|\le\|Q_Hx\|+(2+2\sqrt2)\sqrt{1-x(p)}.$$

Let $z_+,z_-\in B_Z$. If $z=(z_++z_-)/2$ is supported on $H$ and $\|z\|\ge1-\varepsilon$, where $0<\varepsilon<1$, then $$\label{stab:supported-bound}
 \|Q_Hz_\pm\|\le(2+2\sqrt6)\sqrt\varepsilon.$$ More generally, let $R>0$, $d\ge0$, and $h_+,h_-\in Z$ satisfy $\|h_\pm\|\le R$, with $h_++h_-$ supported on $H$. If a common head test $p$, $\rho(p)=1$, satisfies $h_\pm(p)\ge(1-d)R$, then $$\label{stab:common-test-bound}
 \|Q_Hh_\pm\|\le R(2\sqrt{6d}+\sqrt{2d}).$$

Let $z_1,z_2\in B_Z$, $z=(z_1+z_2)/2$, and let $H$ be finite ancestral. For a head test $p$, put $\delta=1-z(p)$ and $\kappa=\|(z(e_g))_{g\in G_H}\|_2$. Then $$\label{stab:gate-original-bound}
 \|Q_Hz_i\|\le2\kappa+(6\sqrt2+6)\sqrt\delta.$$ In particular, choosing a norming head test gives $$\label{stab:original-crossing-bound}
 \|Q_Hz_i\|
 \le2\|Q_Hz\|+(6\sqrt2+6)\sqrt{1-\|P_Hz\|}
 \le2\|Q_Hz\|+15\sqrt{1-\|P_Hz\|}.$$

Let $0<e<1/4$, let $x\in Z$ satisfy $\|x\|=1$, and let $H$ be finite ancestral with $\|Q_Hx\|\le e$. Suppose $p=P_Hp$, $\rho(p)=1$, and $x(p)\ge1-e$. For every $u\in\ker P_H$, $$\label{stab:small-center-implication}
 \max\{\|x+u\|,\|x-u\|\}\le1+e
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \|u\|\le25\sqrt e.$$

Let $A\subset T_\infty$ be finite and ancestral, let $x\in B_\infty$ be supported in $A$, and let $z\in\ker P_A$. Write $$r=\|x\|,\qquad L_+=\|x+z\|,\qquad L_-=\|x-z\|,
 \qquad L=\frac{L_++L_-}{2}.$$ Then $L_+,L_-\ge r$ and $$\label{molw:lens}
 \|z\|\le C\sqrt{L(L-r)},\qquad C=\sqrt2+\sqrt6.$$

Let $H$ be finite and ancestor-closed, $x=P_Hx$, and $x+h,x-h\in B_X$, with arbitrary $h\in X$. Suppose $0<d<1$ and $\|x\|\ge1-d$. For every $\eta>0$, choose finite represented-atom laws with means $z_\pm$ such that $\|z_\pm-(x\pm h)\|\le\eta$. Such laws exist, and every such pair gives $$\label{mola:conditional-prelimit}
 \|Q_Hh\|\le\eta+2\sqrt{d+\eta}
       +2\sqrt{(\eta+\sqrt{2(d+\eta)})^2+4(d+\eta)}.$$ Consequently, $$\|Q_Hh\|\le(2+2\sqrt6)\sqrt d.$$

Let $\delta>0$ and $x\in X$ satisfy $\|x\|=1$. There are a finite ancestral head $H$ and a test $p=P_Hp\in J$ such that $$\label{molb:unscaled-head}
 \rho(p)\le1,\qquad x(p)\ge1-\delta,\qquad
 \|Q_Hx\|\le\delta.$$ For this same head and every $y\in X$ satisfying $\|x+y\|,\|x-y\|\le1+\delta$, put $h=6\delta+\delta^2$. Then $$\label{molb:unscaled-bound}
 \max\{\|Q_H(x+y)\|,\|Q_H(x-y)\|,\|Q_Hy\|\}
 \le F(\delta):=
 \delta+\sqrt{2h}+2\sqrt{(2\delta+\sqrt h)^2+2h}.$$ In particular, for every $\varepsilon>0$ one can choose $\delta>0$ independently of $x$ so that this head satisfies $\|Q_Hy\|<\varepsilon$ for every such $y$.

For $0<\lambda<1$ and $x\in B_X$ with $\|x\|\ge1-\lambda$, there is a finite ancestral set $H$, depending only on $x$ and $\lambda$, such that every $y\in X$ with $x\pm y\in B_X$ satisfies $$\label{molx:actual-center-fourth-bound}
 \|Q_Hy\|\le G_{\mathrm{orig}}(\lambda)
 :=\lambda+2\sqrt{6\lambda}
 +2\sqrt{(2\lambda+\sqrt{6\lambda})^2
                  +12\lambda+4\sqrt{6\lambda}}.$$ In particular $G_{\mathrm{orig}}(\lambda)\to0$ as $\lambda\downarrow0$.

For $0<t<1$, set $\delta=(t/25)^2$. If $x_0=P_Hx_0$ and $y$ are unit vectors, $P_Hy=0$, and both are finitely supported, then $$\label{stab:coef:finite-strict}
 \max\{\|x_0+ty\|,\|x_0-ty\|\}>1+\delta.$$ For general unit $y\in\ker P_H$, the same inequality holds with $\ge$ in place of $>$. Moreover, $$\label{stab:coef:conditional-modulus-eq}
 \widehat\delta_Z(t)\ge t^2/2500.$$

For $0<h<1/4$ and every unit $x\in X$, there is a finite ancestor-closed $H$, depending only on $x,h$, such that $\|x\pm y\|\le1+h$ implies $$\label{molb:eq:matching-lens}
 \|Qy\|\le h+(1+h)
  \bigl[h+2\sqrt{6h}+2(2h+3\sqrt{6h})\bigr].$$

Suppose $Px=x$, $Qy=y$, and $\|x\pm y\|\le1$. Then $$\label{molb:eq:target-sign-bound}
 \|y\|\le(2\sqrt2+2\sqrt6)\sqrt{1-\|x\|}
                         \le8\sqrt{1-\|x\|}.$$ Consequently, if $\|p\pm u_j\|\le1$ and $\|u_i-u_j\|\ge\varepsilon>0$ for all distinct $i,j$ in an infinite sequence, then $$\label{molb:eq:target-separated}
 \|p\|\le1-\varepsilon^2/256.$$

Let $H$ be finite and ancestral, $x=P_Hx$ with $\|x\|=1$, and $u\in\ker P_H$. For every $L>1$, $$\label{stab:sign:parent-lens-bound}
 \max\{\|x+u\|,\|x-u\|\}<L
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 \|u\|\le
 3\left(\sqrt{L^2-1}
            +2\sqrt{\frac{1-L^{-2}}2}\right).$$

Let $P$ be a bounded finite-rank projection on a Banach space and let $(u_j)$ be an infinite bounded sequence. For every $\varepsilon>0$ there are distinct $i,j$ with $\|P(u_i-u_j)\|<\varepsilon$. If in addition $x\pm u_j\in R B$ and $\|u_i-u_j\|\ge\theta$ for all distinct indices, then $v=(u_i-u_j)/2$ satisfies $$\label{mol:half-difference}
 x\pm v\in R B,\qquad \|Pv\|<\varepsilon/2,
 \qquad \|v\|\ge\theta/2.$$

Let $x\in X$ and let $(z_j)\subset X$ satisfy $x\pm z_j\in B_X$ and $z_j(e_v)\to0$ for every forest vertex $v$. Then, with $C=\sqrt2+2\sqrt6<7$, $$\label{molx:coordinate-null-bound}
 \limsup_{j\to\infty}\|z_j\|
 \le C\sqrt{1-\|x\|}\le7\sqrt{1-\|x\|}.$$
